# Useful Design

The one design skill. It carries three things: the craft law that decides what never ships, the Useful brand system that is the default look, and a reference library for every visual job (pages, components, mockups, diagrams, infographics, decks, study guides, copy, tokens).

It does not carry the multi-session product redesign track (`ux-flow-redesign`), the print PDF pipeline (`premium-persian-pdf`), or the creative-coding tool docs (`references/creative-media.md` routes to those).

## Brand default, and the one way to override it

- `references/brand-useful.md` is the default look for most projects: monochrome, minimal, premium, evidence first.
- **If the user explicitly asks for another look, palette, or typeface, use theirs.** The tokens step aside; every other rule in this skill still applies. State in one line which system you are following.
- **Useful Apply is the standing exception.** It keeps its own palette and branding. Never apply Useful tokens to it and never harmonise it toward this system without an explicit decision from Wasim.

## When to use

Any time you build, restyle, review, or mock up a visual surface: a screen, component, dashboard, internal tool, landing or pricing page, deck, prototype, poster, diagram, infographic, study guide, or design-system file. Also when you choose a palette, typeface, layout, spacing or motion, when you write UI or marketing copy, and when you write a prompt for an image model to produce UI.

## The seven non-negotiables

1. **Pin the subject before you touch code.** Product, user, the screen's single job, and internal tool versus external product. One sentence each. If you cannot write it, you are not ready to design it.
2. **Obey an existing system.** If the project has tokens, a theme file, or a design section in its rules, follow it exactly and design only what it leaves open. Never introduce a second visual language into one product.
3. **The blacklist is not advisory.** No purple, violet or indigo themes. No gradient decoration, glow, glass, or grain (a 1 to 3% luminance sheen on a primary fill or composer is finish, not decoration: see `references/app-polish.md`). No emoji icons. No badge pills above headings. No bento reflex. No cards inside cards. No dark hero with a radial glow. No animated-everything. Every banned pattern and its test is in `references/design-law.md`.
4. **Hierarchy in grayscale, one accent, one signature.** Size, weight and spacing carry hierarchy; colour comes last. One accent for primary actions and active state; semantic colour only on real state. Spend boldness in exactly one place per product. Before delivery, remove one accessory.
5. **Design the seven states, not the happy path.** Empty, loading, error, hover, focus-visible, active, disabled. A screen without them is not designed.
6. **The accessibility floor ships silently.** 4.5:1 text contrast, 3:1 on controls, visible focus rings, semantic markup, labelled icon buttons, 40px+ targets, reduced motion respected. Never announced as a feature.
7. **Verify before you claim done.** Real content (never Lorem, never "John Doe"), at least one overflow case, checked at 360px, 390px and 1440px, keyboard pass, and the code sweep for banned patterns. Screenshot your own output and critique it once. A passing desktop screenshot does not establish mobile correctness.

## Read the right reference first

Read the matching reference **in full** with the file-reading tool before writing code. Paths are relative to this skill's own directory.

| Job | Read |
|---|---|
| Any UI code: screen, component, page, restyle | `references/design-law.md`, then `references/interaction-patterns.md` and `references/charts.md` |
| Any product app (web, desktop or mobile): depth, radius, dark theme, labels, platform chrome | `references/app-polish.md` |
| The Useful brand look: tokens, fonts, shell, components | `references/brand-useful.md`, then `references/motion.md` |
| Landing page, marketing page, deck, prototype, HTML artifact | `references/web-artifacts.md` |
| Headlines, hero, CTAs, UI strings, copy polish | `references/copy.md` |
| Appearance-only polish of an existing app, no behaviour change | `references/polish-pass.md`, then `references/app-polish.md` |
| A named product's design language as a reference point | `references/design-systems.md` plus `templates/design-systems/<brand>.md` |
| Token spec or design-system package | `references/design-tokens.md` |
| Architecture, cloud, flow or sequence diagram | `references/diagrams.md` |
| Infographic or poster-style one-pager | `references/infographics.md` plus `templates/infographics/` |
| Study guide, explainer, ELI5, learning document | `references/study-guides.md`, then `references/widget-catalog.md`, then `references/design-law.md` for the code sweep |
| Chat, agent or any transcript that grows under a composer | `references/interaction-patterns.md` — the **Chat transcripts and scroll** section, before writing any of it |
| Motion behaviour and page transitions | `references/motion.md` |
| Generative visuals, animation, ASCII, video, image generation | `references/creative-media.md` |
| Full product UX overhaul over several sessions | the separate `ux-flow-redesign` skill |
| Print-ready A4 PDF | the separate `premium-persian-pdf` skill |

`references/interaction-patterns.md` and `references/charts.md` are the recipe shelf. They are required reading whenever you write UI code, not optional background.

## Verification gate

State plainly which of these you ran and which you skipped:

- Real content in the project's language, with the longest realistic string tested and one overflow case.
- The seven states present, or each absence a stated decision.
- 360px, 390px and 1440px checked; keyboard flow works; focus is visible.
- Contrast passes, reduced motion respected, no layout hidden to conceal a bug.
- Code sweep over changed files for the banned patterns, each hit justified or removed.
- One screenshot, judged frozen: would a stranger guess a human designed this? Then remove one accessory.

## What lives in the sibling skills

