     1	---
     2	title: "Dynamic Capabilities"
     3	description: "Resolve models, subagents, connections, tools, skills, and instructions at runtime with defineDynamic resolver events."
     4	---
     5	
     6	`defineDynamic` resolves the model, subagents, connections, tools, skills, and instructions at runtime from a session event instead of declaring them up front. Reach for it when the right capability isn't known until the session starts, because it hinges on who the caller is, what tenant they belong to, feature flags, or external data. The [subagents](../subagents), [connections](../connections), [tools](../tools), [skills](../skills), and [instructions](../instructions) guides each point here for their dynamic form.
     7	
     8	eve evaluates a dynamic definition module once during compilation to classify and validate it, then retains that module as a runtime entry so its event handlers can run. Its top-level code therefore runs in both phases; keep caller-specific work inside the handlers. See [Authored module lifecycle](../reference/typescript-api#authored-module-lifecycle).
     9	
    10	## Dynamic models
    11	
    12	The `model` field in `agent.ts` accepts `defineDynamic({ events })`. Resolvers
    13	run at `session.started`, `turn.started`, or `step.started` (precedence: step >
    14	turn > session). Every matching handler must return a concrete model. A
    15	missing, invalid, or throwing selection fails the turn before model-dependent
    16	work begins. Prefer `session.started` — prompt caches are per model, so
    17	switching mid-session re-ingests the conversation at uncached prices. See
    18	[agent configuration](../agent-config#choose-the-model-dynamically) for the
    19	full contract.
    20	
    21	Dynamic models do not compile a default model or model metadata. When a
    22	resolver first selects a model, eve normalizes the selection and resolves any
    23	omitted context-window metadata from the AI Gateway catalog. Dynamic connections,
    24	tools, skills, instructions, and subagents may return `null` to omit a capability.
    25	
    26	### Route image inputs to a vision model
    27	
    28	Use `step.started` when model choice depends on the current messages. This
    29	keeps GLM for text and switches to Gemini Flash when user history contains an
    30	image:
    31	
    32	```ts title="agent/agent.ts"
    33	import { defineAgent, defineDynamic } from "eve";
    34	
    35	export default defineAgent({
    36	  model: defineDynamic({
    37	    events: {
    38	      "step.started": (_event, ctx) => {
    39	        const hasImage = ctx.messages.some(
    40	          (message) =>
    41	            message.role === "user" &&
    42	            Array.isArray(message.content) &&
    43	            message.content.some(
    44	              (part) =>
    45	                part.type === "image" ||
    46	                (part.type === "file" &&
    47	                  (part.mediaType === "image" || part.mediaType.startsWith("image/"))),
    48	            ),
    49	        );
    50	
    51	        return hasImage ? "google/gemini-3.5-flash" : "zai/glm-5.2";
    52	      },
    53	    },
    54	  }),
    55	});
    56	```
    57	
    58	eve stages byte-backed `file` parts under `/workspace/attachments` before
    59	`step.started`, but keeps their media type in `ctx.messages`. When an image
    60	reaches the provider, vision models can process it and non-vision models reject
    61	it. eve does not reroute automatically. See [Inbound
    62	attachments](../sandbox#inbound-attachments).
    63	
    64	## Dynamic subagents
    65	
    66	Wrap a declared subagent's own `agent.ts` in `defineDynamic` when its
    67	availability depends on the caller, tenant, environment, or a feature flag.
    68	Return the child definition to configure and expose it. Return `null` to omit
    69	it from the parent's model-visible tools.
    70	
    71	```ts title="agent/subagents/finance/agent.ts"
    72	import { defineAgent, defineDynamic } from "eve";
    73	
    74	export default defineDynamic({
    75	  events: {
    76	    "session.started": (_event, ctx) =>
    77	      ctx.session.auth.current?.attributes.plan === "enterprise"
    78	        ? defineAgent({
    79	            description: "Analyze financial and accounting data.",
    80	            model: "openai/gpt-5.5",
    81	          })
    82	        : null,
    83	  },
    84	});
    85	```
    86	
    87	eve always compiles the subagent's filesystem resources, including its
    88	instructions, tools, skills, connections, sandbox, and nested subagents. It
    89	does not compile an agent config or placeholder model for a dynamic subagent.
    90	When the resolver selects the subagent, eve combines the returned config with
    91	those resources before starting the child session. Each resolution can return
    92	a different model or other runtime agent settings. A returned local config
    93	must use a static model; it cannot contain another `defineDynamic` model.
    94	Runtime-selected models must use string model IDs. Put build configuration on
    95	the outer `defineDynamic` definition; build and Workflow-world configuration
    96	cannot be selected in a handler result.
    97	
    98	A single-file remote subagent uses the same lifecycle. Return
    99	`defineRemoteAgent(...)` to expose the selected deployment, or `null` to omit it:
   100	
   101	```ts title="agent/subagents/finance.ts"
   102	import { defineDynamic, defineRemoteAgent } from "eve";
   103	
   104	export default defineDynamic({
   105	  events: {
   106	    "session.started": (_event, ctx) =>
   107	      ctx.session.auth.current?.attributes.plan === "enterprise"
   108	        ? defineRemoteAgent({
   109	            description: "Analyze financial and accounting data.",
   110	            url: "https://finance-agent.example.com",
   111	          })
   112	        : null,
   113	  },
   114	});
   115	```
   116	
   117	The returned remote definition can change its URL, path, headers, auth,
   118	principal forwarding, and output schema. Function-valued URLs resolve when the
   119	dynamic event runs. Auth and headers remain lazy and resolve before each
   120	outbound request without entering durable workflow state.
   121	
   122	Dynamic subagents support `session.started` and `turn.started`. A turn selection
   123	shadows the session selection for that turn, including when the turn handler
   124	returns `null`. If a resolver throws or returns an invalid definition, eve logs the
   125	failure and omits the subagent.
   126	
   127	The resolved set applies to local and remote direct delegation. Background subagents are not
   128	exposed inside the model-authored `Workflow` tool. eve
   129	also checks availability again before starting the child, so a stale or
   130	manually constructed call fails with `SUBAGENT_UNAVAILABLE`. Treat conditional
   131	availability as capability composition, not as the only authorization
   132	boundary: sensitive child tools still need their own authorization and
   133	approval checks.
   134	
   135	## Dynamic connections
   136	
   137	Use a dynamic connection when the available MCP servers or OpenAPI services
   138	depend on the authenticated caller. A handler returns one
   139	`defineMcpClientConnection(...)` or `defineOpenAPIConnection(...)`, a map of
   140	connection definitions, or `null`. Wrap every returned connection in its
   141	protocol helper. Connection resolvers receive `ctx.session` and
   142	`ctx.channel.kind`; they do not receive conversation messages, delivery
   143	payloads, tool inputs, model outputs, continuation tokens, or free-form channel
   144	metadata. Select accounts and endpoints from authenticated session identity or
   145	application-owned data.
   146	
   147	This example exposes one MCP connection for each cloud account enabled for the
   148	current user:
   149	
   150	```ts title="agent/connections/accounts.ts"
   151	import { defineDynamic, defineMcpClientConnection } from "eve/connections";
   152	import { listEnabledAccounts, mintAccountToken } from "../lib/accounts";
   153	
   154	export default defineDynamic({
   155	  events: {
   156	    "session.started": async (_event, ctx) => {
   157	      const principal = ctx.session.auth.current;
   158	      if (principal?.principalType !== "user") return null;
   159	
   160	      const accounts = await listEnabledAccounts(principal);
   161	      return Object.fromEntries(
   162	        accounts.map((account) => [
   163	          account.slug,
   164	          defineMcpClientConnection({
   165	            url: "https://mcp.cloud.example.com",
   166	            description: `${account.label} (${account.accountId})`,
   167	            instanceKey: account.accountId,
   168	            auth: {
   169	              principalType: "user",
   170	              getToken: ({ principal }) => mintAccountToken(principal, account),
   171	            },
   172	          }),
   173	        ]),
   174	      );
   175	    },
   176	  },
   177	});
   178	```
   179	
   180	The returned definitions use the same auth, headers, filtering, provided
   181	arguments, and approval options as static [MCP](../connections/mcp) and
   182	[OpenAPI](../connections/openapi) connections. Each resolved connection joins
   183	the per-step connection registry, appears in `connection_search`, and exposes
   184	discovered tools as `<connection>__<tool>`.
   185	
   186	Set `instanceKey` on every authenticated dynamic connection. Use a stable,
   187	non-secret account or tenant identifier, and change it whenever the endpoint,
   188	account, or auth provider changes. eve hashes the value before storing the
   189	resolved instance identity in durable authorization state. If a parked sign-in
   190	callback resumes after the resolver selects a different instance, eve rejects
   191	the callback instead of passing it to the new connection or reusing its token.
   192	
   193	### Naming and conflicts
   194	
   195	| Return shape                  | File                            | Connection name(s)      |
   196	| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
   197	| single connection definition  | `agent/connections/accounts.ts` | `accounts`              |
   198	| map `{ production, staging }` | `agent/connections/accounts.ts` | `production`, `staging` |
   199	
   200	A map key must be a legal connection name: lowercase ASCII letters, digits,
   201	and dashes, starting with a letter, up to 64 characters. Map keys are bare;
   202	eve does not prefix them with the file slug. A dynamic connection overrides a
   203	same-named static connection. Two effective dynamic resolvers cannot emit the
   204	same name; namespace one map key to remove the ambiguity.
   205	
   206	### Events and recovery
   207	
   208	Dynamic connections support `session.started` and `turn.started`. A turn result
   209	replaces that file's session result for the turn, including when the turn
   210	handler returns `null`. A throwing or invalid handler fails the lifecycle
   211	without rebuilding the registry, so a static connection shadowed by the
   212	dynamic result cannot reappear as a fallback.
   213	
   214	eve may run the active session and turn handlers again when a parked turn
   215	resumes or a durable step retries. This rebuilds live auth, header, approval,
   216	and provided-argument callbacks without serializing them into workflow state.
   217	Keep connection resolvers idempotent, and keep external side effects outside
   218	the handler.
   219	
   220	## Dynamic tools
   221	
   222	Pass `defineDynamic` an `events` object whose handlers return either a single `defineTool(...)`, a `Record<string, defineTool(...)>`, or `null` for no tools. Wrap every entry in `defineTool()`. eve records durable descriptors for `execute`, approval request and response policies, input-scoped `approvalKey` callbacks, and `toModelOutput`, so a parked call can reconstruct the same callbacks in a fresh process.
   223	
   224	Dynamic tool executors receive the same `ToolContext` as static authored tools, including inline provider auth through `ctx.getToken(provider)` and `ctx.requireAuth(provider)`.
   225	
   226	The example below builds one tool per warehouse table. A map return names each tool by its bare key, so the model sees `orders`, `users`, and so on.
   227	
   228	```ts title="agent/tools/query.ts"
   229	import { defineDynamic, defineTool } from "eve/tools";
   230	import { z } from "zod";
   231	import { listTables, runReadOnly } from "../lib/warehouse";
   232	
   233	export default defineDynamic({
   234	  events: {
   235	    "session.started": async (_event, ctx) =>
   236	      Object.fromEntries(
   237	        (await listTables()).map((t) => [
   238	          t.name,
   239	          defineTool({
   240	            description: `Query ${t.name}. Columns: ${t.columns.join(", ")}`,
   241	            inputSchema: z.object({ sql: z.string() }),
   242	            execute: ({ sql }) => runReadOnly(t.name, sql),
   243	          }),
   244	        ]),
   245	      ),
   246	  },
   247	});
   248	```
   249	
   250	### Author replayable callbacks
   251	
   252	Write callback properties as inline function expressions, arrows, method shorthand, or module-level function references. eve transforms authored modules that import `defineTool`, including helper modules outside `agent/tools/`, and stores each callback's referenced closure values independently.
   253	
   254	Closure values must be JSON-serializable. Plain objects, arrays, strings, finite numbers, booleans, and `null` are supported; `undefined` object properties are omitted. Functions, class instances, `Date`, `Map`, symbols, non-finite numbers, and cyclic values fail resolution with the tool name and callback phase instead of being serialized lossily.
   255	
   125	    paths: {
   126	      "/api/{cartId}/items/{itemId}": {
   127	        get: {
   128	          operationId: "getCartItem",
   129	          parameters: [
   130	            {
   131	              name: "cartId",
   132	              in: "path",
   133	              required: true,
   134	              schema: { type: "string" },
   135	            },
   136	            {
   137	              name: "itemId",
   138	              in: "path",
   139	              required: true,
   140	              schema: { type: "string" },
   141	            },
   142	          ],
   143	          responses: { "200": { description: "OK" } },
   144	        },
   145	      },
   146	    },
   147	  },
   148	});
   149	```
   150	
   151	The parameter `name` must exactly match the placeholder inside the path. If the spec omits an `in: "path"` parameter, the generated tool has no input for that segment and eve cannot fill it in from query parameters.
   152	
   153	## Schema annotations
   154	
   155	eve removes `default` and `example` annotations when their values conflict with the declared schema type. For example, a boolean property with `default: "false"` loses that annotation, while `default: false` is retained. This prevents invalid examples from guiding model input; eve does not coerce annotation values.
   156	
   157	## Approval gates
   158	
   159	Generated operations can mutate state like authored tools. Use the shared connection `approval` option for human approval, and combine it with `operations.allow` for the smallest practical surface. See [Per-connection approval](../connections#per-connection-approval) for the helpers and [Human-in-the-loop](../human-in-the-loop#approvals) for policy behavior.
   160	
   161	## What to read next
   162	
   163	- [Connections](../connections): shared auth, headers, approval, and per-caller patterns.
   164	- [MCP connections](./mcp): connect to remote MCP servers.
   165	- [Authentication](../guides/auth-and-route-protection): establish the caller identity required by user-scoped auth.
   166	- [Security model](../concepts/security-model): how connection credentials stay out of the model's reach.
     1	---
     2	title: "Build a Memory Provider"
     3	description: "Implement the recall, capture, and tools contract so any store or memory service can back an eve memory slot."
     4	---
     5	
     6	A memory provider is an object with a `recall` handler and optional `capture`
     7	and `tools` handlers. eve calls those handlers at fixed points in the agent
     8	lifecycle and passes each one a locked scope key, the projected conversation,
     9	and a stable operation ID. Anything that can read and write under that key can
    10	be a provider. Package one as a library that exports a provider factory, or
    11	write one directly inside an agent.
    12	
    13	```ts title="agent/lib/notes-memory.ts"
    14	import { defineMemoryProvider } from "eve/memory";
    15	import { defineTool } from "eve/tools";
    16	import { z } from "zod";
    17	import { notes } from "./notes-db";
    18	
    19	export function notesMemory() {
    20	  return defineMemoryProvider({
    21	    recall: {
    22	      async "turn.started"(ctx) {
    23	        const rows = await notes.search({
    24	          partition: ctx.memory.scope.key,
    25	          query: ctx.turn.input,
    26	          limit: 5,
    27	        });
    28	        return {
    29	          messages: rows.map((row) => ({ id: row.id, content: row.text })),
    30	        };
    31	      },
    32	    },
    33	    capture: {
    34	      async "turn.completed"(ctx) {
    35	        await notes.ingest({
    36	          partition: ctx.memory.scope.key,
    37	          idempotencyKey: ctx.operationId,
    38	          messages: ctx.messages,
    39	        });
    40	      },
    41	    },
    42	    async tools(ctx) {
    43	      return {
    44	        forget: defineTool({
    45	          description: "Delete a remembered note by its ID.",
    46	          inputSchema: z.object({ id: z.string() }),
    47	          async execute({ id }) {
    48	            await notes.delete({ partition: ctx.memory.scope.key, id });
    49	            return { deleted: true };
    50	          },
    51	        }),
    52	      };
    53	    },
    54	  });
    55	}
    56	```
    57	
    58	Bind the provider to a slot like any other:
    59	
    60	```ts title="agent/memory/notes.ts"
    61	import { defineMemory } from "eve/memory";
    62	import { byPrincipal } from "eve/memory/scope";
    63	import { notesMemory } from "../lib/notes-memory";
    64	
    65	export default defineMemory({
    66	  description: "Notes the caller has asked the agent to remember.",
    67	  provider: notesMemory(),
    68	  scope: byPrincipal,
    69	});
    70	```
    71	
    72	The model sees the tool as `notes__forget`, and the slot description is
    73	prepended to its description.
    74	
    75	## The provider contract
    76	
    77	`defineMemoryProvider()` accepts three surfaces. Omit any handler the provider
    78	does not need; `fileMemory()`, for example, implements recall and tools but no
    79	capture.
    80	
    81	| Surface   | Handlers                                              | Responsibility                                           |
    82	| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
    83	| `recall`  | `"turn.started"` (required), `"compaction.completed"` | Return messages to place in model context                |
    84	| `capture` | `"turn.completed"`, `"compaction.requested"`          | Observe settled history and write to the store           |
    85	| `tools`   | one function                                          | Return model-facing operations bound to the locked scope |
    86	
    87	### Operation context
    88	
    89	Every handler receives a `MemoryOperationContext`:
    90	
    91	| Field                    | Meaning                                                                                                    |
    92	| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    93	| `memory.scope.key`       | Opaque, versioned digest of the namespace and scope. Use it as the partition key for every read and write. |
    94	| `memory.scope.namespace` | The resolved namespace string                                                                              |
    95	| `memory.scope.value`     | The resolved scope string or tuple                                                                         |
    96	| `memory.slot`            | The path-derived slot name                                                                                 |
    97	| `messages`               | Projected conversation history for this phase                                                              |
    98	| `operationId`            | Stable per session, sequence, phase, and slot. Use it as an idempotency key.                               |
    99	| `abortSignal`            | Cancellation for the operation                                                                             |
   100	| `session`                | Session ID, authentication, and other `SessionContext` fields                                              |
   101	
   102	Phase-specific fields:
   103	
   104	- `turn.started` and `turn.completed` add `turn` with the turn `id`, `input`
   105	  messages, and `sequence`.
   106	- `compaction.requested` adds `compaction.modelId` and
   107	  `compaction.usageInputTokens`; `turn` is `null` for standalone compaction.
   108	- `compaction.completed` adds `compaction.modelId`; `turn` may be `null`.
   109	
   110	`tools()` receives a `MemoryToolsContext` with the same `memory` and `turn`
   111	fields plus the ordinary dynamic-resolve context.
   112	
   113	### Recall results
   114	
   115	A recall handler returns `{ messages }`, `null`, or `undefined`. Each message
   116	has `content` and an optional `id`:
   117	
   118	```ts
   119	return {
   120	  messages: [
   121	    { id: "preferred-language", content: "The user prefers Spanish." },
   122	    { content: "A relevant note without a stable identity." },
   123	  ],
   124	};
   125	```
   126	
   127	eve adds each message to model context as a user-role message attributed to the
   128	slot. Provider content is never promoted to system instructions.
   129	
   130	Use a stable `id` for replaceable facts. A later message with the same ID in
   131	the same slot, namespace, and scope supersedes the earlier one; identical
   132	content is a no-op. Messages without an ID accumulate, even when their content
   133	repeats. Omitting an earlier ID from a later result does not delete it; recall
   134	cannot retract, only supersede.
   135	
   136	### Tools
   137	
   138	`tools()` returns a map of `defineTool()` values or `null`. eve qualifies each
   139	key as `<slot>__<key>`; the qualified name must start with a letter, contain
   140	only letters, digits, underscores, or dashes, and be at most 64 characters.
   141	Schemas, `approval`, `outputSchema`, and `toModelOutput` work as they do for
   142	authored tools.
   143	
   144	A tool closes over the locked scope for the current turn, so it cannot be
   145	redirected to another tenant or caller by the model. eve keeps each tool
   146	callback replayable after a process restart or redeployment.
   147	
   148	## Lifecycle
   149	
   150	| Phase                  | Handler                           | `messages` contains                                        |
   151	| ---------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
   152	| `turn.started`         | `recall["turn.started"]`          | History before recall; the new delivery is in `turn.input` |
   153	| `turn.completed`       | `capture["turn.completed"]`       | Settled history after a successful turn                    |
   154	| `compaction.requested` | `capture["compaction.requested"]` | History before the checkpoint changes                      |
   155	| `compaction.completed` | `recall["compaction.completed"]`  | The checkpoint plus canonical recalled records             |
   156	
   157	At turn start, eve resolves and locks the scope for every active slot before any
   158	recall runs. All slots see the same pre-recall history, and eve commits their
   159	validated results atomically.
   160	
   161	During compaction, eve excludes recalled records from the summarizer, keeps the
   162	latest value for each keyed record plus every unkeyed record, and then calls
   163	`recall["compaction.completed"]` against the new checkpoint. This keeps
   164	provider content attributable and prevents a summary from turning it into
   165	ordinary conversation history. If raw superseded records exceed 512 entries or
   166	256 KiB, eve canonicalizes them without waiting for the normal token threshold;
   167	this changes session history only, not the provider's store.
   168	
   169	Calling `clear()` on a session removes its history, recalled records, locked
   170	scopes, and replay bookkeeping. It does not touch the provider's store; a later
   171	turn recalls the same data again.
   172	
   173	## Failure behavior
   174	
   175	- A throwing or invalid `recall["turn.started"]` fails the turn before the
   176	  model call. No slot's recall results are committed. If the turn's
   177	  `abortSignal` is already aborted, eve treats the error as cancellation and
   178	  continues with any queued steering replacement. An `AbortError` with an active
   179	  signal still fails the turn.
   180	- A throwing `capture["compaction.requested"]` leaves history unchanged.

