   361	
   362	Resolvers across files run concurrently.
   363	
   364	## Dynamic skills
   365	
   366	A dynamic skills file resolves which [skill](../skills) a caller can load, keyed on the principal. It resolves on `session.started` and `turn.started` only (`step.started` is reserved for dynamic tools). Read `ctx.session.auth` or channel metadata and return a `defineSkill(...)` (named after the file slug) or `null`:
   367	
   368	```ts title="agent/skills/team_playbook.ts"
   369	import { defineDynamic, defineSkill } from "eve/skills";
   370	import { PLAYBOOKS } from "../lib/playbooks";
   371	
   372	export default defineDynamic({
   373	  events: {
   374	    "session.started": (_event, ctx) => {
   375	      const team = ctx.session.auth.current?.attributes.team;
   376	      const markdown = team ? PLAYBOOKS[team] : undefined;
   377	      return markdown ? defineSkill({ markdown }) : null;
   378	    },
   379	  },
   380	});
   381	```
   382	
   383	The caller's team gets its own playbook advertised as a loadable skill; everyone else gets nothing.
   384	
   385	Skills follow the same naming rule as tools: a single `defineSkill(...)` is named after the file slug, while a map names each entry by its bare key (namespace the key yourself if it might collide). A dynamic skill overrides a same-named authored one; two dynamic resolvers emitting the same name throws.
   386	
   387	## Dynamic instructions
   388	
   389	A dynamic instructions file returns `defineInstructions({ content, role? })` built from the principal, tenant, channel, or external data. Omit `role` for system context:
   390	
   391	```ts title="agent/instructions/persona.ts"
   392	import { defineDynamic, defineInstructions } from "eve/instructions";
   393	
   394	export default defineDynamic({
   395	  events: {
   396	    "session.started": (_event, ctx) => {
   397	      const plan = ctx.session.auth.current?.attributes.plan ?? "free";
   398	      return defineInstructions({
   399	        content: `The caller is on the ${plan} plan. Match the depth of your answers to it.`,
   400	      });
   401	    },
   402	  },
   403	});
   404	```
   405	
   406	Use `role: "user"` when the resolved value is application or user context that should become part of durable history:
   407	
   408	```ts title="agent/instructions/brief.ts"
   409	import { defineDynamic, defineInstructions } from "eve/instructions";
   410	import { loadBrief } from "../lib/briefs";
   411	
   412	export default defineDynamic({
   413	  events: {
   414	    "turn.started": async (_event, ctx) => {
   415	      const brief = await loadBrief(ctx.session.auth.current);
   416	      return brief ? defineInstructions({ content: brief, role: "user" }) : null;
   417	    },
   418	  },
   419	});
   420	```
   421	
   422	Instruction resolvers support `session.started` and `turn.started` only. A system result lives in that scope and stays outside history. A user result is appended to history at the lifecycle boundary, with session results before turn results and both before the current delivery. There is no automatic deduplication: returning the same user content on a later turn intentionally appends another message.
   423	
   424	Resolver snapshots reflect that order. At `session.started`, `ctx.messages` includes static user-role instructions. At `turn.started`, it also includes user-role results from `session.started`. These augmented snapshots are specific to instruction resolvers; tools, skills, models, and subagents keep their existing message snapshots.
   425	
   426	Returning `null` or blank content contributes nothing. A throwing or invalid session resolver leaves any wider valid system selection in place. Every turn starts with fresh turn-scoped system instructions, so a failed or empty turn result cannot leak the previous turn's value. Completed lifecycle steps are replay-safe: parking, resuming, or replaying them does not duplicate user-role messages.
   427	
   428	Dynamic system content that changes frequently can reduce provider prompt-cache reuse. Prefer session scope for stable values and use turn scope only when the context must be refreshed. Cache behavior remains provider-specific.
   429	
   430	## What to read next
   431	
   432	- Conditionally expose a specialist → [Subagents](../subagents)
   433	- Resolve caller-specific external services → [Connections](../connections)
   434	- The static tool basics this builds on → [Tools](../tools)
   435	- The built-in tools and how to override them → [Built-in tools](../concepts/built-in-tools)
   436	- Authenticate a tool or connection to an external service → [Auth & route protection](./auth-and-route-protection)
   437	- Durable per-session memory for resolvers to read → [State](../concepts/state)
   438	- Cross-session recall and provider-generated tools → [Memory](../memory)
   145	
   146	An `auth` resolver returns the same provider object accepted by static `auth`, including a `connect(...)` provider for interactive OAuth. The resolver itself can be async. Header callbacks can return the whole map, as above, or resolve individual values:
   147	
   148	```ts
   149	headers: {
   150	  "X-Tenant-Id": (ctx) => tenantId(ctx.session.auth.current),
   151	}
   152	```
   153	
   154	Use `principalType: "user"` for per-user tokens so eve rejects unauthenticated callers and keys its step-local token cache by user. The resolver supplements route auth; it does not authenticate the inbound request. Static auth objects and header maps remain available when every caller shares the same configuration.
   155	
   156	## Per-connection approval
   157	
   158	To put every tool a connection serves behind a human, use the helpers from `eve/tools/approval`:
   159	
   160	```ts title="agent/connections/linear.ts"
   161	import { defineMcpClientConnection } from "eve/connections";
   162	import { once } from "eve/tools/approval";
   163	
   164	export default defineMcpClientConnection({
   165	  url: "https://mcp.linear.app/mcp",
   166	  description: "Linear workspace.",
   167	  auth: { getToken: async () => ({ token: process.env.LINEAR_API_TOKEN! }) },
   168	  approval: once(),
   169	});
   170	```
   171	
   172	`never()` lets every call through, `once()` asks for approval the first time in a session, and `always()` asks every time. The pause and resume is the same human-in-the-loop flow covered in [Tools](/docs/tools).
   173	
   174	For connection tools that can create, modify, delete, transmit, purchase, message, or access sensitive data, use approval, allow-lists, or other safeguards appropriate to the action.
   175	
   176	## Interactive OAuth via Vercel Connect
   177	
   178	When the server uses OAuth and you want each end-user to sign in through their own browser, turn on interactive authorization with [Vercel Connect](https://vercel.com/docs/connect). The `connect()` helper from `@vercel/connect/eve` handles consent, encrypted token storage, and refresh, then hooks all of that into eve's authorization flow.
   179	
   180	Create and attach the connector from the Vercel project or agent app that will use it:
   181	
   182	```bash
   183	npm install @vercel/connect
   184	vercel link
   185	vercel connect create <service> --name <name>
   186	vercel connect attach <connector-uid> --yes
   187	vercel env pull
   188	```
   189	
   190	Use the connector UID returned by the CLI in `connect("<connector-uid>")`. The service identifier accepted by `vercel connect create` depends on the provider; it is not necessarily the same as an MCP runtime URL or OpenAPI base URL.
   191	
   192	```ts title="agent/connections/linear.ts"
   193	import { connect } from "@vercel/connect/eve";
   194	import { defineMcpClientConnection } from "eve/connections";
   195	
   196	export default defineMcpClientConnection({
   197	  url: "https://mcp.linear.app/mcp",
   198	  description: "Linear workspace: issues, projects, cycles, and comments.",
   199	  auth: connect("linear/myagent"),
   200	});
   201	```
   202	
   203	Use the connector UID returned by `vercel connect create`; the display name passed to `--name` is not the UID. `connect("linear/myagent")` is shorthand for a user-scoped interactive OAuth connection: eve resolves a token for the active user before each tool call, emits `authorization.required` when that user has not authorized yet, and resumes the parked turn after the callback completes.
   204	
   205	When a local subagent needs interactive authorization, eve surfaces the authorization lifecycle on the root session's channel, including through nested subagent chains. The challenge still points to the child session's callback, so completing it resumes the child directly while the parent continues waiting for its result.
   206	
   207	That means the channel that creates or continues the session must authenticate a real user. For a web app, configure `agent/channels/eve.ts` so your app session maps to `principalType: "user"`; for platform channels, use the built-in channel auth that maps the sender to a user principal. If no authenticated user is attached to the session, the first user-scoped connection call fails with `reason: "principal_required"`.
   208	
   209	If the remote service should act as the agent itself instead of the end-user, make the Connect connection app-scoped:
   210	
   211	```ts title="agent/connections/linear.ts"
