     1	---
     2	title: "Client SDK"
     3	description: "Call an eve agent from TypeScript with Client, sessions, auth, and health checks."
     4	---
     5	
     6	The `eve/client` entrypoint is the typed client for eve's default HTTP API. Use it from scripts, server-to-server integrations, tests, evals, backend jobs, or custom UIs that want the session protocol without hand-writing the POST and NDJSON (newline-delimited JSON) stream loop.
     7	
     8	For browser chat UIs, start with [`useEveAgent`](../frontend/overview). For wire-level details, read [Sessions, runs & streaming](../../concepts/sessions-runs-and-streaming). The client sits between those two: lower level than the frontend hooks, higher level than raw HTTP.
     9	
    10	## Create a client
    11	
    12	A `Client` binds one host, auth policy, and header policy:
    13	
    14	```ts
    15	import { Client } from "eve/client";
    16	
    17	const client = new Client({
    18	  host: "http://127.0.0.1:2000",
    19	});
    20	```
    21	
    22	`host` is the URL where the eve routes are mounted. In a same-origin browser integration this is often `""`; scripts and backend services usually name the full URL. Any query parameters on `host` are included on every request, including session POSTs and event streams. Request-specific parameters, such as a stream cursor, take precedence when names overlap.
    23	
    24	## Check health
    25	
    26	Use `health()` when a script needs to fail early before creating a session:
    27	
    28	```ts
    29	const health = await client.health();
    30	console.log(health.status, health.workflowId);
    31	```
    32	
    33	The client requires the successful response to match `{ ok: true, status: "ready", workflowId: string }`. Non-2xx responses throw `ClientError`, which carries the HTTP `status` and response `body`; invalid JSON or a malformed successful payload throws `HealthResponseError`.
    34	
    35	## Inspect an agent
    36	
    37	Use `info()` to inspect an agent. The client requires the complete agent-info version 4 response before returning it:
    38	
    39	```ts
    40	const info = await client.info();
    41	console.log(info.agent.name, info.agent.model.id);
    42	```
    43	
    44	Version 4 separates active static definitions from dynamic resolvers, includes binding-backed source ownership and composition history, reports first-class memory slots and their provider-tool wrapper dependencies, reports local and remote agents separately, and returns the exact compiled channel route order. A non-success response throws `ClientError`; invalid JSON, an earlier schema version, duplicate identities, inconsistent totals, or mismatched source provenance throws `AgentInfoResponseError`.
    45	
    46	## Authentication
    47	
    48	Pass `auth` when the [eve channel](../../channels/eve) route requires credentials:
    49	
    50	```ts
    51	const client = new Client({
    52	  host: "https://agent.example.com",
    53	  auth: {
    54	    bearer: async () => await getAccessToken(),
    55	  },
    56	});
    57	```
    58	
    59	Bearer values and Basic auth passwords can be strings or functions. Functions run before every HTTP call, including stream reconnects:
    60	
    61	```ts
    62	const client = new Client({
    63	  host: "https://agent.example.com",
    64	  auth: {
    65	    basic: {
    66	      username: "agent-client",
    67	      password: async () => await getRotatingSecret(),
    68	    },
    69	  },
    70	});
    71	```
    72	
    73	For a Vercel OIDC-protected deployment, use `vercelOidc`. The client resolves the token once per request and sends it as both the bearer credential and Vercel's trusted-OIDC header:
    74	
    75	```ts
    76	import { getVercelOidcToken } from "@vercel/oidc";
    77	
    78	const client = new Client({
    79	  host: "https://agent.example.com",
    80	  auth: {
    81	    vercelOidc: {
    82	      token: async () => await getVercelOidcToken(),
    83	    },
    84	  },
    85	});
    86	```
    87	
    88	Use `headers` for route-specific credentials such as bypass tokens or tenant hints. Like `auth`, it can be static or dynamic:
    89	
    90	```ts
    91	const client = new Client({
    92	  host: "https://agent.example.com",
    93	  headers: async () => ({
    94	    "x-vercel-protection-bypass": await getBypassToken(),
    95	  }),
    96	  redirect: "manual",
    97	});
    98	```
    99	
   100	Set `redirect` to `"manual"` or `"error"` on credential-bearing clients so fetch cannot forward custom authorization headers to another origin. The policy applies to inspection requests, custom fetches, session creation, and event streams.
   101	
   102	Per-request headers can be attached to an individual turn:
   103	
   104	```ts
   105	const response = await session.send("Run the check.", {
   106	  headers: { "x-request-id": requestId },
   107	});
   108	
   109	await response.result();
   110	```
   111	
   112	Per-request headers override client-level values with the same name. For example, a turn can set its application user's `Authorization` header while `vercelOidc` continues to send the deployment-protection credential in `x-vercel-trusted-oidc-idp-token`.
   113	
   114	## Sessions
   115	
   116	For an ID-addressed session, create it explicitly with the first message:
   117	
   118	```ts
   119	const { session, response } = await client.sessions.create({ message: "Summarize account A." });
   120	await response.result();
   121	
   122	await (await session.send("Now list the risks.")).result();
   123	await session.compact();
   124	await session.clear();
   125	```
   126	
   127	If you already know the durable ID, attach a fixed handle without performing I/O:
   128	
   129	```ts
   130	const session = client.sessions.attach("wrun_A");
   131	```
   132	
   133	The fixed client handle exposes the full lifecycle around that ID. The calls below show the available shapes independently:
   134	
   135	```ts
   136	const response = await session.send("Continue the analysis.");
   137	await response.result();
   138	
   139	await session.cancel({ turnId: "turn_123" });
   140	await session.compact();
   141	await session.clear();
   142	
   143	for await (const event of session.stream({ follow: false })) {
   144	  console.log(event.type);
   145	}
   146	
   147	await session.reset({ reason: "User requested a fresh session" });
   148	```
   149	
   150	`client.sessions` stores only the session ID and stream cursor. Every method calls an ID-addressed `/eve/v1/session/:sessionId/...` route. Sending through a handle for an unknown or terminal ID fails instead of creating a replacement, and reset leaves the handle pinned to the retired ID.
   151	
   152	A client can own many independent fixed sessions at once:
   153	
   154	```ts
   155	const { session: alice, response: aliceResponse } = await client.sessions.create({
   156	  message: "Summarize account A.",
   157	});
   158	const { session: bob, response: bobResponse } = await client.sessions.create({
   159	  message: "Summarize account B.",
   160	});
